      "text": "We start with the equation $\\mathbf{M}^T\\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{I}.$  We have\n\\[\\mathbf{M}^TM = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\\\ 2y & y & -y \\\\ z & -z & z \\end{pmatrix}. \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} x^2 + x^2 + x^2 & x(2y) + y(2y) - y(-y) & xz + xz + zz \\\\ x(2y) + y(2y) - y(-y) & (2y)^2 + y^2 + (-y)(-y) & yz + yz - zz \\\\ xz + xz + zz & yz + yz - zz & z^2 + (-y)(-y) + z^2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3x^2 & 3x^2 & 3y^2 \\\\ 3x^2 & 3y^2 & 3z^2 \\\\ 3y^2 & 3z^2 & 2z^2 + y^2 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Then we have\n\\[\\mathbf{M}^T\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3x^2 & 3x^2 & 3y^2 \\\\ 3x^2 & 3y^2 & 3z^2 \\\\ 3y^2 & 3z^2 & 2z^2 + y^2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]So we have $3x^2 = 1,$ $3y^2 = 1,$ and $3z^2 + y^2 = 1.$  Then $3(x^2 + y^2) = 3.$  Since $x^2 + y^2 \\ge \\,\\frac12,$ then $x^2 + y^2 = \\boxed{\\frac13}.$",
