    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "For the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ to satisfy $\\mathbf{M}^T\\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{I},$ the matrix $\\mathbf{M}^T\\mathbf{M}$ must be the identity matrix. We can find the transpose of $\\mathbf{M}$:\n\\[\\mathbf{M}^T = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & z \\\\ 2y & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\nThen, we can calculate the matrix $\\mathbf{M}^T\\mathbf{M}=$\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & z \\\\ 2y & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Since the matrix $\\mathbf{M}^T\\mathbf{M}$ must be the identity matrix, the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{M}^T\\mathbf{M} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ must be the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}.$  This means that $\\mathbf{M}$ is an orthogonal matrix, so $\\mathbf{M}$ is either a rotation, reflection, or diagonal, that is, its columns are all unit vectors. This gives us the following possibilities:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{M} &= \\mathbf{Z} := \\left(\\begin{array}{ccc} \\cos \\lambda &  \\sin \\lambda & 0 \\\\ - \\sin \\lambda & + \\cos \\lambda & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{array}\\right), \\\\\n\\mathbf{M} &= \\mathcal{T} := \\left(\\begin{array}{ccc} \\cos \\theta & \\sin \\theta & 0 \\\\ - \\sin \\theta & + \\cos \\theta & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{array}\\right),\n\\end{align*}\nfor $\\lambda$ or $\\theta$ an arbitrary real number. Next, suppose that $\\mathbf{M} = \\mathcal{T}.$ Then, the columns of $\\mathcal{T}$ are $\\begin{pmatrix} \\cos \\theta \\\\ - \\sin \\theta \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix},$ $\\begin{pmatrix} \\sin \\theta \\\\ + \\cos \\theta \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix},$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix},$ and we must have\n\\[\\left(\\frac{\\cos \\theta}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{- \\sin \\theta}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta}{4} = \\frac{1}{4}.\\]Thus, $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\boxed{1}.$ The answer is 1",
