      "text": "First, we compute $\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M}.$\n\\[\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\\\ 2y & y & -y \\\\ z & -z & z \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2x^2 & 0 \\\\ 2x^2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2x^2 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\nSince $\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{I},$ we must have $2x^2 = 1$ and $2y^2 = 0$ and $2z^2 = 0.$  Therefore, $x = \\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},$ $y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}},$ and $z = 0.$  Then $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\boxed{1}.$The answer is: 1",
