      "text": "We have that\n\\[\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Then\n\\[\\mathbf{M}^T = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\\\ 2y & y & -y \\\\ z & -z & z \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Then\n\\[\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & x & x \\\\ 2y & y & -y \\\\ z & -z & z \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2y & z \\\\ x & y & -z \\\\ x & -y & z \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} x^2 + zx + zx & x(2y) + xy + x(-y) & xz + y(-y) + z(-z) & y(x) + 2y(-y) + z(z) \\\\ 2xy + y^2 - yx & 2y(2y) + y^2 + 2y(-y) & 2yz - yz - yz & (z)(x) + (-z)(2y) + (z)(-(-y)) \\\\ zx + x(-z) + z(x) & zx + x(-y) - z(y) & zx + x(z) + z(z) & z(2y) + (-z)(y) + z(y) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} x^2 + 2zx & xy & -y^2 - z^2 \\\\ 2xy + y^2 - yx & 2y^2 + 2y^2 - 2y^2 & 2yz - yz - yz \\\\ zx + x(-z) + z(x) & zx + x(-y) - z(y) & zx + z^2 + z^2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} x^2 + 2zx & xy & -y^2 - z^2 \\\\ 2xy + y^2 - yx & 0 & 0 \\\\ zx + x(-z) + z(x) & zx + x(-y) - z(y) & 2z^2 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Setting this equal to $\\mathbf{I},$ we have\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} x^2 + 2zx & xy & -y^2 - z^2 \\\\ 2xy + y^2 - yx & 0 & 0 \\\\ zx + x(-z) + z(x) & zx + x(-y) - z(y) & 2z^2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]By comparing entries, we see that $x^2 + 2zx = 1,$ $xy = 0,$ and $-y^2 - z^2 = 0.$ Since $xy = 0,$ either $x = 0$ or $y = 0.$ Suppose $x = 0.$ Then $-y^2 - z^2 = 0,$ so $y^2 + z^2 = 0.$ Since $y^2$ and $z^2$ are nonnegative, this means $y = z = 0.$\n\nWe also have $x^2 + 2zx = 1,$ so $0 + 0 = 1,$ which is a contradiction, so these hypotheses do not hold. Thus, $y = 0,$ and then $-y^2 - z^2 = 0,$ so $y^2 + z^2 = 0.$ Since $y^2$ and $z^2$ are nonnegative, this means $y = z = 0.$\n\nIf $x = 0$ and $y = z = 0,$ then $\\mathbf{M}^T \\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{I}.$\n\nTherefore, $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\boxed{0}.$. The answer is 0",
